> Hand-check two categorical targets and one continuous coordinate

Take two generated-prior categories, targets0 and1, with zero logits for both rows.
The categorical CE is log2. Give Q continuous means zero and choose algebra targets
u=(-1,1). With fixed unit variance, the per-row Gaussian NLL is

\[
 R_c=\operatorname{mean}[-\log p_c],\quad
 R_u=\tfrac12\operatorname{mean}\sum_j(u_j-\mu_j)^2+\tfrac U2\log(2\pi),\quad R=R_c+R_u.
\]

Here U=1, so R_u=(1+log(2π))/2 and R≈2.112086. Q's mean derivatives are(.5,-.5),
and the category-logit derivatives are(-.25,.25) and(.25,-.25). Targets also have live
continuous derivatives, although the fitting loop updates only selected parameter stores.
The softmax convenience field is not consumed by the loss: CE uses logits directly.

For independent uniform category C and uniform[-1,1] coordinates, fixed prior entropy
is log(C)+U log2. The native `lower_bound` field computes that constant minus R;
it is approximately-.725791 in this deliberately chosen two-row fixture. This is algebra,
not a random-sample measurement of mutual information. In the experiment, batch averaging
is a Monte Carlo score of the variational objective, without a finite-sample guarantee on
true mutual information. A negative score never implies negative true mutual information.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/infogan_toy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n  = dm::nn;
  namespace m  = n::models;
  auto       t = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto c = n::tensor({0, 1}, {2}, n::DType::Int64), target = n::tensor({-1, 1}, {2, 1}, t);
  std::vector<double> x, recognition, bound;
  for (int k = -40; k <= 40; ++k) {
    x.push_back(k / 20.);
    m::InfoRecognition q{n::zeros({2, 2}, t), {}, n::full({2, 1}, k / 20., t)};
    auto               loss = m::InfoGanRecognitionLoss(q, c, target);
    recognition.push_back(loss.total.item());
    bound.push_back(loss.lower_bound.item());
  }
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.lines(x, recognition, "Recognition R", {37, 99, 235}, 2.)
      .lines(x, bound, "Fixed entropy minus R", {220, 38, 38}, 2.)
      .y_limits(-3.3, 6)
      .x_label("Shared predicted continuous mean; targets -1,+1")
      .y_label("Nats per generated row")
      .title("Unit variance: recognition NLL and its entropy-offset score")
      .save_svg("build/infogan_toy.svg");
  m::InfoRecognition q{
      n::zeros({2, 2}, t).requires_grad_(), {}, n::zeros({2, 1}, t).requires_grad_()};
  auto loss        = m::InfoGanRecognitionLoss(q, c, target);
  auto derivatives = n::grad(loss.total, {q.logits, q.mean});
  std::print(
      "| fixture quantity | value |\n|---|---:|\n| categorical CE |{:.9f}|\n| continuous NLL "
      "|{:.9f}|\n| total R |{:.9f}|\n| entropy minus R |{:.9f}|\n",
      loss.categorical.item(),
      loss.continuous.item(),
      loss.total.item(),
      loss.lower_bound.item());
  std::print(
      "Logit gradients: {}; mean gradients: {}.\n",
      derivatives[0].values_str(),
      derivatives[1].values_str());
}
